## Part 1: Literature Review & Related Work
Target Tracks: NeurIPS (AI Safety/Alignment) & ICML (Data-Centric AI/System Optimization)
## 1.1 Multi-Agent Orchestration and Sequential Alignment
Early frameworks for multi-agent interaction focused heavily on structural conversation topologies and role-playing games to solve simple tasks. However, as these architectures moved into enterprise tasks like multi-step data engineering, researchers noted a major problem: sequential token decay. While single-turn alignment algorithms like Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) [3] and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) [4] control immediate token distributions well, they do not account for compounding errors across long agent chains.
Recent studies in agent safety have looked at using an external "LLM-as-a-judge" to evaluate conversational paths. Yet, these auditing routines are often slow and introduce their own stochastic errors into the evaluation loop. This paper addresses this gap by creating an exact, internal mathematical metric—the Semantic Trust Index ($\mathcal{ST_I}$)—that measures information entropy directly within the model's hidden representation spaces, removing the need for an expensive second LLM evaluation step.
## 1.2 Programmatic Prompt Compilers vs. Relational Data Governance
The shift away from static prompt engineering toward declarative prompt optimization frameworks, such as DSPy [6] and LangGraph sub-graphs, treats prompt synthesis like an optimization task. While these compilers improve accuracy on text tasks, running them over massive corporate databases introduces a difficult engineering trade-off.
Traditional database security relies on hardcoded Row-Level Security (RLS) and Column-Level Security (CLS) logic embedded inside data catalogs like Apache Iceberg or Delta Lake. Forcing an LLM compiler to read all of this raw governance metadata quickly floods its context window. This causes high token latency and introduces structural attention blur, which degrades code generation performance.
Prior work has attempted to use keyword searches to filter out unneeded schemas before prompting the model. Our Governance-Aware Semantic Compiler (GASC) goes a step further by modeling access policies as sparse mathematical dependencies. This allows the system to prune prompts dynamically based on active security restrictions, enforcing strict data compliance while keeping token overhead to a minimum.

[Prior Work: Keyword Pruning]   Query ---> Static Word Filter ---> Dense Prompt Context
[Our Work: GASC Matrix Pruning] Query ---> Sparse Policy Array  ---> Minimal Safe Sub-Graph

